---
/* THE COLD OPEN — /black-box only.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a preloader. This page loads in roughly 300ms, so a progress
 * bar here would be measuring nothing and saying so in a way that looked like measurement.
 * On a site whose entire claim is that a machine should not be able to misreport itself,
 * a lying progress indicator is the one ornament we cannot afford. So this is a title
 * sequence: it plays OVER a page that has already finished loading, it never gates content,
 * and it is skippable from the first frame.
 *
 * WHAT IT MUST NOT BREAK.
 *   · The crawler. The document is complete and rendered underneath; this is a fixed overlay
 *     added on top, and it removes itself from the DOM when it ends.
 *   · Reduced motion. A vestibular trigger is not a style preference. Under
 *     prefers-reduced-motion this never runs and never mounts — the console is right there,
 *     and the same four claims are in the page body as text.
 *   · The guided read. ReplayDemo opens its guide the first time the console scrolls into
 *     view, and the console IS in view behind this overlay — so without coordination the
 *     guide would open behind the curtain, burn its once-per-browser showing, and the
 *     reader would surface into a dock mid-sequence. <html data-coldopen> is the interlock:
 *     "running" suppresses the autostart entirely, "done" lets autoplay through but skips
 *     the guide, because one onboarding at a time is the whole point.
 *
 * ONCE PER SESSION. A cover on every navigation turns a site into a slideshow.
 */
---

<div
	class="co"
	data-coldopen-root
	role="region"
	aria-label="Introduction to the Black Box"
	hidden
>
	<canvas class="co-stage" data-co-canvas aria-hidden="true"></canvas>


	<div class="co-copy">
		<p class="co-act" data-co-act></p>
		<p class="co-line" data-co-line></p>
		<p class="co-sub" data-co-sub></p>

		{/* ACT 03. Two statements, verified to source, and an attribution each. The
		    fine-print paragraph that used to sit under them is one short line now: it has
		    to be there, it does not have to be an essay. */}
		<div class="co-quotes" data-co-quotes hidden>
			<figure class="co-quote" data-co-item>
				{/* The whole sentence. Shortening it to "Essentially unprecedented in the
				    history of technology" read better and was a misquotation: it drops the
				    subject and presents a fragment as a complete statement, under a named
				    attribution. Density is not a good enough reason to put words in
				    somebody's mouth that they did not arrange that way. */}
				<blockquote>This lack of understanding is essentially unprecedented in the history of technology.</blockquote>
				<figcaption>Dario Amodei, Anthropic · 2025</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<figure class="co-quote" data-co-item>
				<blockquote>We don’t really understand exactly how they do those things.</blockquote>
				<figcaption>Geoffrey Hinton · 60 Minutes, 2023</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<p class="co-fine" data-co-item>Neither is affiliated with Oxiedo.</p>
		</div>

		{/* ACT 04. A number and four words. The sourcing lives on the page underneath, which
		    is where somebody checking it will go anyway; three lines a cell turned a glance
		    into homework. */}
		<ul class="co-figs" data-co-figs="cost" hidden>
			<li class="co-fig" data-co-fig>
				<span class="co-fig-n">419</span>
				<span class="co-fig-u">interruptions in one 54-day run</span>
			</li>
			<li class="co-fig" data-co-fig>
				<span class="co-fig-n">$15M</span>
				<span class="co-fig-u">of compute burned on it</span>
			</li>
			<li class="co-fig" data-co-fig>
				<span class="co-fig-n">~8%</span>
				<span class="co-fig-u">of medical AI can be updated</span>
			</li>
			<li class="co-fig" data-co-fig>
				<span class="co-fig-n">Aug 2026</span>
				<span class="co-fig-u">EU logging rules apply</span>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</div>

	<footer class="co-foot">
		{/* THE RAIL IS A CONTROL, not a progress bar. Twenty-five seconds is a long time to
		    ask of a stranger, and the answer is not to cut the argument down to something
		    that fits an attention span — it is to let the reader set the pace. A skimmer
		    clicks to the end in two seconds, an investor sits on act three for a minute. */}
		<ol class="co-rail">
			{[
				'The machine', 'The architecture', 'The field’s own words',
				'What it costs', 'What stays locked', 'The break', 'The console',
			].map((name, i) => (
				<li><button type="button" data-co-seg={i} aria-label={`Go to ${name}`}><i></i></button></li>
			))}
		</ol>
		<button type="button" class="co-skip" data-co-skip>
			Skip to the console <kbd>Esc</kbd>
		</button>
	</footer>
</div>

{/* THE GATE RUNS DURING PARSE, before the console below it paints, so there is no frame in
    which the page is visible and the cover is not. It only unhides; the sequence itself is
    in the module script and can take its time arriving. If that module never loads, this
    element is still removed by the fallback timer, so a bundling failure cannot leave a
    reader staring at a black rectangle. */}
<script is:inline>
	(function () {
		var el = document.querySelector('[data-coldopen-root]');
		if (!el) return;
		if (location.search.indexOf('intro=0') > -1) return el.remove();
		try {
			if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return el.remove();
		} catch (e) { /* matchMedia unavailable — play it */ }
		document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-coldopen', 'running');
		el.hidden = false;
		// ONLY FOR THE FIRST LOAD, and only if the module never arrives. Every later run is
		// owned by mount(), which carries its own timer. This one exists so a bundling
		// failure on a cold cache cannot leave a reader facing a black rectangle.
		window.setTimeout(function () {
			if (el.isConnected && el.dataset.coldopenLive !== '1') {
				document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-coldopen', 'done');
				el.remove();
				document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('coldopen:done'));
			}
		}, 20000);
	})();
</script>

<script>
	type Act = { at: number; label: string; line: string; sub: string };

	/* NOT '[data-coldopen]'. The inline gate above stamps data-coldopen="running" on <html>
	   for the ReplayDemo interlock, and <html> precedes this element in document order — so
	   that selector returned documentElement, and root.remove() at the end of the sequence
	   deleted the entire document. The overlay and the flag get separate hooks, permanently. */
	/* EVERY LANDING, NOT ONCE A SESSION.
	 *
	 * Two things had to change together. The session flag is gone, so the sequence plays
	 * whenever someone arrives. And it is now driven by astro:page-load rather than by the
	 * inline gate, because the site navigates with the ClientRouter: on a client-side visit
	 * the body is swapped but the document is not, and Astro deduplicates inline script
	 * execution — so the gate ran on the first arrival and never again. A reader clicking
	 * "Open the Black Box", leaving, and clicking it a second time got nothing at all, which
	 * is a state neither of the two policies was supposed to produce.
	 *
	 * astro:page-load fires on the first load and after every swap, so one path covers both.
	 * mount() is idempotent — data-coldopen-live guards a double boot — and returns its own
	 * teardown, which astro:before-swap calls if someone navigates away mid-sequence.
	 */
	let live: (() => void) | null = null;

	function mount(): (() => void) | null {
		const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-coldopen-root]');
		const canvas = root?.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('[data-co-canvas]') ?? null;
		if (!root || !canvas) return null;         // not this page
		if (root.dataset.coldopenLive === '1') return live;

		/* ?intro=0 SKIPS IT. The sequence runs on every landing, which is right for a
		   visitor and wrong for everyone who opens this page forty times in an afternoon —
		   whoever is building it, whoever is demonstrating the console in a meeting, and
		   the guided-read test suite, which lands here repeatedly and needs the console and
		   not the curtain. A shareable link that goes straight to the instrument is worth
		   having on its own account, and it keeps the tests honest: they exercise the real
		   page rather than a flag invented to get around it. */
		if (new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('intro') === '0') { root.remove(); return null; }

		// Re-read the preference on every run rather than trusting the first load: a reader
		// can turn it on between two visits, and this must honour that immediately.
		let reduced = false;
		try { reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches; }
		catch { /* unavailable */ }
		if (reduced) { root.remove(); return null; }

		root.dataset.coldopenLive = '1';
		document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-coldopen', 'running');
		root.hidden = false;
		{
		const elAct = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-co-act]')!;
		const elLine = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-co-line]')!;
		const elSub = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-co-sub]')!;
		const elFigs = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-co-figs]')!;
		const segs = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-co-seg]')];
		const skip = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-co-skip]')!;

		/* ── the score ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		   Seven acts over ~25s. That is long, and it is long on purpose: the first version
		   ran fourteen seconds and told only the narrow story — a network breaks, ORMAS
		   names the break. The argument this page actually has to make is larger. Opacity
		   is not a fault in a network, it is the arithmetic of how one trains; the field
		   says so in its own words; it has a price and a set of dates attached; and the
		   thing it finally costs is the data nobody will release. None of that fits in
		   fourteen seconds, and none of it should be cut to fit.
		   What makes the length affordable is that the reader steers: the rail is seven
		   buttons, the arrow keys step, and Skip has been in frame one since the first
		   version. Auto-advance is the default, not the only option. */
		const T = {
			ignite: 0,
			machine: 700,
			arch: 3200,
			words: 5700,
			cost: 9000,
			locked: 12200,
			brk: 14900,
			hand: 17400,
			end: 19600,
		};

		type Act = {
			at: number; label: string; line: string; sub: string;
			quotes?: boolean; figs?: 'cost';
		};

		/* ONE LINE, THEN A FRAGMENT. Nothing longer.
		   The previous cut put three-sentence paragraphs on screen — the sort of thing that
		   belongs on the page underneath, where a reader has chosen to read and can take
		   their time. Over a title sequence, at a size you can take in at a glance, a
		   paragraph is not information, it is a wall, and the reader's answer to a wall is
		   to stop looking. Prose went down, type size went up, and the runtime came down
		   from 25.8s to 19.6s on the strength of it. The pictures carry the argument now;
		   the words name it. */
		const ACTS: Act[] = [
			{
				at: T.machine,
				label: '01 — The machine',
				line: 'A network breaks mid-training.',
				sub: 'Nothing inside it can say which part.',
			},
			{
				at: T.arch,
				label: '02 — The architecture',
				line: 'Nothing was hidden.',
				sub: 'Every signal touches every parameter. There was never a part to point at.',
			},
			{
				at: T.words,
				label: '03 — Not a minority view',
				line: '',
				sub: '',
				quotes: true,
			},
			{
				at: T.cost,
				label: '04 — What it costs',
				line: 'The bill is already on the record.',
				sub: '',
				figs: 'cost',
			},
			{
				at: T.locked,
				label: '05 — What stays locked',
				line: 'The best data is never trained on.',
				sub: 'No custodian hands data to a model that cannot say what it took.',
			},
			{
				at: T.brk,
				label: '06 — The break',
				line: 'Four operations. A fixed chain.',
				sub: 'Every component stays addressable for the whole run.',
			},
			{
				at: T.hand,
				label: '07 — The console',
				line: 'One archived run, replayed.',
				sub: 'Every figure below is read from it.',
			},
		];

		/* ── geometry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		   The same vocabulary the console draws in, so act four can resolve INTO the real
		   diagram rather than cut to it: five columns, eleven marks to a stage, the true
		   channel count printed beside a capped column. anatomy.ts holds the original —
		   UNIT_CAP is 11 there and 11 here, and if that ever changes these drift. */
		const COLS = [
			{ n: 3, name: 'input', sub: '32×32×3' },
			{ n: 11, name: '3→32', sub: '960 params' },
			{ n: 11, name: '32→64', sub: '18,624 params' },
			{ n: 11, name: '64→128', sub: '74,112 params' },
			{ n: 10, name: 'output', sub: '10 classes' },
		];
		const NET_W = 900;
		const NET_H = 300;
		const DEAD = 3; // the column that dies in act one — stage 2 in the console's naming

		type Pt = { x: number; y: number };
		const layout: Pt[][] = COLS.map((c, i) => {
			const x = (i / (COLS.length - 1)) * (NET_W - 120) + 60;
			const span = c.n === 3 ? 70 : c.n === 10 ? 150 : 190;
			return Array.from({ length: c.n }, (_, j) => ({
				x,
				y: NET_H / 2 + (c.n === 1 ? 0 : (j / (c.n - 1) - 0.5) * span),
			}));
		});

		// Captured so the closures below keep the narrowing the guard above established.
		const cv = canvas;
		/* WHERE THE COPY STARTS, in curtain coordinates.
		   The diagram used to be placed at a fraction of the viewport height while the copy
		   block is anchored to the bottom, so the two drifted apart as the screen grew: at
		   1500×940 there were 57–115px between the last of the diagram and the first line
		   of type, and at 2000×1225 there were 217–295px — a hole through the middle of the
		   frame, which is what made the control row at the bottom look stranded rather than
		   placed. The diagram hangs off the copy now, so the composition holds at any height.
		   Cached, not read per frame: it is a forced layout, and the answer only changes
		   when the act changes or the window resizes. */
		const elCopy = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.co-copy')!;
		let copyTop = 0;
		function measureCopy() {
			copyTop = elCopy.getBoundingClientRect().top - root!.getBoundingClientRect().top;
		}

		const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
		let dpr = 1;
		let VW = 0;
		let VH = 0;

		function size() {
			// Capped at 2. A 3x phone painting 385 edges a frame at full density spends the
			// whole budget on pixels nobody can resolve.
			dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
			// The overlay's own box, not the viewport — it starts below the nav now, and
			// sizing the canvas to innerHeight drew the sequence one nav taller than the
			// space it had and pushed the type off the bottom.
			const r = root!.getBoundingClientRect();
			VW = Math.round(r.width);
			VH = Math.round(r.height);
			cv.width = Math.round(VW * dpr);
			cv.height = Math.round(VH * dpr);
			cv.style.width = VW + 'px';
			cv.style.height = VH + 'px';
			measureCopy();
		}
		size();
		window.addEventListener('resize', size);

		const ease = (t: number) => (t < 0 ? 0 : t > 1 ? 1 : 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3));
		const clamp01 = (t: number) => (t < 0 ? 0 : t > 1 ? 1 : t);
		const span = (t: number, a: number, b: number) => clamp01((t - a) / (b - a));

		const AMBER = '224,162,51';
		const SLATE = '127,163,220';
		const WARN = '224,100,95';
		const CREAM = '241,239,232';

		/* Where the real console's ORMAS diagram sits, so the network can land on it. If the
		   bundle has not arrived yet there is nothing to land on, and the sequence simply
		   fades instead — a handoff that depends on a fetch cannot be allowed to fail loudly. */
		function target(): { cx: number; cy: number; s: number } | null {
			const svg = document.querySelector('[data-anatomy-ormas] svg');
			if (!svg) return null;
			const r = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
			// Both rects are viewport-relative; the canvas is not, since the overlay is
			// offset by the nav. Subtracting the overlay's own origin puts the target back
			// in canvas space — without this the network landed a nav-height too low.
			const o = root!.getBoundingClientRect();
			if (r.width < 40 || r.bottom < o.top || r.top > o.bottom) return null;
			return {
				cx: r.left + r.width / 2 - o.left,
				cy: r.top + r.height / 2 - o.top,
				s: r.width / NET_W,
			};
		}

		function draw(t: number) {
			ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
			ctx.clearRect(0, 0, VW, VH);

			// ── ignition: one mark, then the field ──────────────────────────────────
			const ig = span(t, 0, T.machine);

			// where the network sits, and how big
			// The network holds the screen for acts one and two, recedes behind the words and
			// the figures, and comes back for the break.
			const recede = span(t, T.words - 250, T.words + 350);
			const restore = span(t, T.brk - 350, T.brk + 250);
			const back = Math.max(0, recede - restore);
			const land = ease(span(t, T.hand + 300, T.end - 150));
			const tgt = land > 0 ? target() : null;

			const baseS = Math.min(1, (VW - 80) / NET_W) * (1 - 0.14 * back);
			const baseCx = VW / 2;
			// Sit the diagram just above the copy, clamped so it can neither ride up under
			// the masthead on a short window nor sink into the type on a tall one.
			const halfH = (NET_H / 2) * baseS;
			const baseCy = Math.max(halfH + 34, Math.min(copyTop - 56 - halfH, VH * 0.52));
			const cx = tgt ? baseCx + (tgt.cx - baseCx) * land : baseCx;
			const cy = tgt ? baseCy + (tgt.cy - baseCy) * land : baseCy;
			const s = tgt ? baseS + (tgt.s - baseS) * land : baseS;

			/* NOTHING ON THIS SCREEN IS EVER COMPLETELY STILL.
			   Acts three to five froze solid — the pulse loop was gated behind the recede,
			   the draw-in was long finished and the break had not started, so twelve of the
			   twenty-five seconds were a static JPEG with text sliding over it. A frozen
			   instrument reads as a crashed instrument.
			   Three things run underneath everything now: a breath on the scale, a scan
			   passing through the network, and the signal itself, which never stops. All
			   three are pure functions of t, like everything else in here, because seek()
			   depends on a jump backwards replaying an act exactly rather than approximately. */
			const breathe = 1 + Math.sin(t / 2600) * 0.006 * (1 - land);

			// The scan is the "this is powered" signal, and it costs one gradient and one
			// line. It crosses in a little over five seconds so it is always somewhere.
			const SWEEP_MS = 5200;
			const sweepX = -140 + ((t % SWEEP_MS) / SWEEP_MS) * (NET_W + 280);

			ctx.save();
			ctx.translate(cx, cy);
			ctx.scale(s * breathe, s * breathe);
			ctx.translate(-NET_W / 2, -NET_H / 2);

			// 0.82 left the network at 0.18 and it simply disappeared behind act two's
			// figures. It is meant to recede, not to exit.
			const dim = 1 - 0.62 * back;
			const drawn = ease(span(t, T.machine - 200, T.machine + 1100));
			const killed = span(t, T.machine + 2500, T.machine + 2900);
			/* ACT TWO'S ONE VISUAL IDEA. The dim all-to-all wash — every error signal
			   reaching every parameter — comes up to full while the readable lattice drops
			   away, so for three seconds the screen shows the smear and nothing else. That
			   smear is the argument: there is no component left in there to put a question
			   to. It is the only moment in the sequence where the picture is deliberately
			   illegible, because illegibility is the point being made. */
			const flood = span(t, T.arch - 200, T.arch + 900) * (1 - span(t, T.words - 700, T.words));
			const costBeat = span(t, T.cost - 200, T.cost + 300) * (1 - span(t, T.locked - 500, T.locked));
			const sweepLit = drawn * (1 - land);
			const opened = span(t, T.brk + 300, T.brk + 1500);

			/* ── edges, drawn in two passes ──────────────────────────────────────────
			   One pass of every-to-every at very low alpha, and one of a near-neighbour
			   lattice on top at four times that. A single all-to-all pass at a legible
			   alpha is what the first version did, and 385 straight lines between two
			   eleven-mark columns at this width moiré into a grey smear — the eye reads
			   texture, not structure.

			   It also happens to be the true picture. The dim wash IS the network: in a
			   standard arm every error signal reaches every parameter, which is exactly
			   why no component can be isolated. The bright lattice is only what a reader
			   can follow. Density as the argument, rather than density as decoration. */
			for (let c = 0; c < layout.length - 1; c++) {
				const a = layout[c];
				const b = layout[c + 1];
				const reach = clamp01(drawn * (layout.length - 1) - c);
				if (reach <= 0) continue;
				const dead = killed > 0 && (c === DEAD - 1 || c === DEAD);
				// The repair has to reach the edges too. Restoring the marks to amber while
				// their connections stayed dead drew a stage that was named but still severed.
				const fade = dead ? 1 - 0.78 * killed * (1 - opened) : 1;

				ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${(0.05 + 0.20 * flood) * fade * dim})`;
				ctx.lineWidth = 0.6 + 0.25 * flood;
				ctx.beginPath();
				for (const p of a) {
					for (const q of b) {
						ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y);
						ctx.lineTo(p.x + (q.x - p.x) * reach, p.y + (q.y - p.y) * reach);
					}
				}
				ctx.stroke();

				ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${0.2 * (1 - 0.75 * flood) * fade * dim})`;
				ctx.lineWidth = 0.9;
				ctx.beginPath();
				for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
					const p = a[i];
					const j = Math.round((i / Math.max(1, a.length - 1)) * (b.length - 1));
					for (const k of [j - 1, j, j + 1]) {
						const q = b[k];
						if (!q) continue;
						ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y);
						ctx.lineTo(p.x + (q.x - p.x) * reach, p.y + (q.y - p.y) * reach);
					}
				}
				ctx.stroke();
			}

			/* ── signal, flowing left to right and indifferent to the damage ─────────
			   The `back < 0.6` that used to be on this line is what killed acts three to
			   five. Behind the reading acts the flow is quieter and slower — it must not
			   compete with a quotation — but it does not stop, because the one thing this
			   picture must never say is that the run has ended. */
			if (drawn > 0.6) {
				const phase = (t / (1400 + 1100 * back)) % 1;
				const pulseA = 0.3 * (1 - 0.4 * back);
				for (let c = 0; c < layout.length - 1; c++) {
					const a = layout[c];
					const b = layout[c + 1];
					const f = (phase + c * 0.12) % 1;
					const lit = f > 0.5 ? 1 - (f - 0.5) * 2 : f * 2;
					if (lit <= 0.02) continue;
					ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${pulseA * lit * dim})`;
					ctx.lineWidth = 1.1;
					ctx.beginPath();
					for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i += 2) {
						const p = a[i];
						const q = b[Math.min(i, b.length - 1)];
						ctx.moveTo(p.x + (q.x - p.x) * Math.max(0, f - 0.12), p.y + (q.y - p.y) * Math.max(0, f - 0.12));
						ctx.lineTo(p.x + (q.x - p.x) * f, p.y + (q.y - p.y) * f);
					}
					ctx.stroke();
				}
			}

			// ── the scan ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
			const sweepOn = drawn * (1 - land);
			if (sweepOn > 0.02) {
				const g = ctx.createLinearGradient(sweepX - 74, 0, sweepX + 16, 0);
				g.addColorStop(0, `rgba(${SLATE},0)`);
				g.addColorStop(0.78, `rgba(${SLATE},${0.055 * sweepOn * dim})`);
				g.addColorStop(1, `rgba(${SLATE},0)`);
				ctx.fillStyle = g;
				ctx.fillRect(sweepX - 74, NET_H / 2 - 152, 90, 304);
				ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${0.09 * sweepOn * dim})`;
				ctx.lineWidth = 1;
				ctx.beginPath();
				ctx.moveTo(sweepX, NET_H / 2 - 152);
				ctx.lineTo(sweepX, NET_H / 2 + 152);
				ctx.stroke();
			}

			/* ── act five: the data that cannot get in ───────────────────────────────
			   The one act whose motion is an argument rather than atmosphere. Sources
			   arrive from outside, reach a boundary and are turned back — which is the
			   act's own sentence, drawn: the data is not secret and nobody is careless,
			   it simply cannot be let in. Deterministic from t and the index, so stepping
			   back to this act replays it rather than restarting it somewhere else. */
			const lockOn = span(t, T.locked - 250, T.locked + 500)
				* (1 - span(t, T.brk - 800, T.brk - 250));
			if (lockOn > 0.02) {
				const wallX = layout[0][0].x - 54;
				ctx.setLineDash([3, 7]);
				ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${AMBER},${0.2 * lockOn})`;
				ctx.lineWidth = 1;
				ctx.beginPath();
				ctx.moveTo(wallX, NET_H / 2 - 150);
				ctx.lineTo(wallX, NET_H / 2 + 150);
				ctx.stroke();
				ctx.setLineDash([]);

				for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
					const seed = (i * 0.61803398875) % 1;
					const dur = 2400 + seed * 1500;
					const ph = ((t + seed * 4000) % dur) / dur;
					const y = NET_H / 2 + (seed - 0.5) * 258 + Math.sin(i * 2.7) * 12;
					const travel = Math.min(1, ph / 0.72);
					const x = -175 + (wallX + 175) * travel;
					const hit = clamp01((ph - 0.72) / 0.28);
					const a = lockOn * (1 - hit) * (0.3 + 0.55 * travel);
					if (a <= 0.012) continue;
					ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${SLATE},${a})`;
					ctx.beginPath();
					ctx.arc(x - hit * 9, y, 2.2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
					ctx.fill();
					if (hit > 0.02 && hit < 0.98) {
						ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${AMBER},${lockOn * (1 - hit) * 0.55})`;
						ctx.lineWidth = 1.1;
						ctx.beginPath();
						ctx.arc(wallX, y, 3 + hit * 10, -0.85, 0.85);
						ctx.stroke();
					}
				}
			}

			// ── the sealed volume, and the seam it breaks along ─────────────────────
			if (t > T.brk - 400 && land < 1) {
				const box = ease(span(t, T.brk - 400, T.brk + 200));
				const split = ease(span(t, T.brk + 500, T.brk + 1600));
				const pad = 26;
				const x0 = layout[1][0].x - pad;
				const x1 = layout[3][0].x + pad;
				const y0 = NET_H / 2 - 120;
				const y1 = NET_H / 2 + 120;
				const mid = (x0 + x1) / 2;
				ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
				for (const [a, b, dir] of [[x0, mid, -1], [mid, x1, 1]] as [number, number, number][]) {
					ctx.save();
					ctx.translate(dir * split * 170, 0);
					ctx.globalAlpha = box * (1 - split) * (1 - split);
					ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},0.30)`;
					ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(7,8,15,0.88)';
					ctx.beginPath();
					ctx.rect(a, y0, b - a, y1 - y0);
					ctx.fill();
					ctx.stroke();
					ctx.restore();
				}
				if (split > 0 && split < 1) {
					const g = ctx.createLinearGradient(mid - 40, 0, mid + 40, 0);
					g.addColorStop(0, `rgba(${AMBER},0)`);
					g.addColorStop(0.5, `rgba(${AMBER},${0.5 * Math.sin(split * Math.PI)})`);
					g.addColorStop(1, `rgba(${AMBER},0)`);
					ctx.fillStyle = g;
					ctx.fillRect(mid - 40, y0, 80, y1 - y0);
				}
			}

			// ── marks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
			layout.forEach((col, c) => {
				const reach = clamp01(drawn * (layout.length - 1) - (c - 1));
				if (reach <= 0) return;
				const isStage = c >= 1 && c <= 3;
				const isDead = c === DEAD;
				col.forEach((p) => {
					let fill: string;
					let r = 3.1;
					// ORDER MATTERS. `opened` is tested before `isDead`, because act three is
					// the stage being named and repaired — leaving it red through the break
					// says the opposite of the act it appears in, and it did.
					if (isStage && opened > 0) {
						fill = `rgba(${AMBER},${(0.2 + 0.75 * opened) * dim})`;
						r = 3.1 + 0.5 * opened;
					} else if (isDead && killed > 0) {
						// A slow beat while the cost is on screen. The failure is not a past
						// event being reported, it is still running up the bill in act four —
						// and a stage that had simply stopped moving read as resolved.
						const flash = killed < 1 ? Math.sin(killed * Math.PI) : 0;
						const beat = costBeat * (0.5 + 0.5 * Math.sin(t / 620));
						fill = `rgba(${WARN},${(0.22 + 0.6 * flash + 0.3 * beat) * dim})`;
					} else {
						fill = `rgba(${SLATE},${0.82 * dim})`;
					}
					if ((isStage && opened > 0.2) || (isDead && killed > 0 && killed < 1)) {
						ctx.shadowBlur = 11 * (isDead ? Math.sin(killed * Math.PI) : opened);
						ctx.shadowColor = isDead ? `rgba(${WARN},0.9)` : `rgba(${AMBER},0.75)`;
					}
					ctx.fillStyle = fill;
					ctx.beginPath();
					ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
					ctx.fill();
					ctx.shadowBlur = 0;

					// The scan lights what it crosses. Without this the sweep is a bar
					// sliding over a picture; with it, the picture is being read.
					const touched = sweepLit * Math.max(0, 1 - Math.abs(p.x - sweepX) / 58);
					if (touched > 0.02) {
						ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${0.55 * touched * dim})`;
						ctx.beginPath();
						ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, r + 1.1 * touched, 0, Math.PI * 2);
						ctx.fill();
					}
				});
			});

			// ── names, only once the box is open: the point of the act ──────────────
			if (opened > 0.35 && land < 0.7) {
				const a = (opened - 0.35) / 0.65 * (1 - land / 0.7);
				ctx.font = '600 10px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace';
				ctx.textAlign = 'center';
				for (let c = 1; c <= 3; c++) {
					ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${AMBER},${0.95 * a})`;
					ctx.fillText(`stage ${c - 1}`, layout[c][0].x, NET_H / 2 - 108);
					ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${0.45 * a})`;
					ctx.fillText(COLS[c].sub, layout[c][0].x, NET_H / 2 + 136);
				}
				ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${AMBER},${0.5 * a})`;
				ctx.fillRect(layout[1][0].x - 26, NET_H / 2 + 152, layout[3][0].x - layout[1][0].x + 52, 1);
				ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${CREAM},${0.55 * a})`;
				ctx.font = '400 9.5px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace';
				ctx.fillText(
					'one shared bottleneck · the chain cannot grow with the network',
					NET_W / 2,
					NET_H / 2 + 168,
				);
			}

			// ── act one's caption: the absence, stated where it happens ─────────────
			if (killed > 0.6 && back < 0.4 && t < T.brk - 300) {
				const a = clamp01((killed - 0.6) / 0.4) * (1 - back / 0.4)
					* (1 - clamp01((t - (T.brk - 1200)) / 900));
				ctx.font = '400 10px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace';
				ctx.textAlign = 'center';
				ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${WARN},${0.85 * a})`;
				ctx.fillText('no per-component quantity is computed here', NET_W / 2, NET_H / 2 + 150);
			}

			ctx.restore();

			// ── the first mark, before anything else exists ─────────────────────────
			if (ig < 1) {
				const a = 1 - ease(span(t, 250, T.machine));
				ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(${AMBER},${a})`;
				ctx.beginPath();
				ctx.arc(VW / 2, VH * 0.4, 3 + 2 * Math.sin(t / 120), 0, Math.PI * 2);
				ctx.fill();
			}
		}

		/* ── type ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
		const quotes = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-co-quotes]')!;
		const figSets = new Map<string, HTMLElement>(
			[...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-co-figs]')]
				.map((el) => [el.dataset.coFigs!, el]),
		);

		let shown = -1;
		function paintType(t: number) {
			let idx = -1;
			for (let i = 0; i < ACTS.length; i++) if (t >= ACTS[i].at - 150) idx = i;
			if (idx !== shown) {
				shown = idx;
				const a = ACTS[idx];
				root!.classList.remove('is-typing');
				void root!.offsetWidth; // restart the entrance
				root!.classList.add('is-typing');
				elAct.textContent = a ? a.label : '';
				elLine.textContent = a ? a.line : '';
				elSub.textContent = a ? a.sub : '';
				elSub.hidden = !a || !a.sub;
				quotes.hidden = !a?.quotes;
				for (const [name, el] of figSets) el.hidden = a?.figs !== name;
				// The block just changed height — quotes, a four-cell grid or neither.
				measureCopy();
				segs.forEach((seg, i) => seg.classList.toggle('is-on', i <= idx));
			}

			// Items inside an act stagger in across the act's own length, so a four-cell
			// readout fills rather than appearing at once, and a reader who steps straight
			// to an act still gets everything it holds.
			const a = ACTS[idx];
			if (a) {
				const to = ACTS[idx + 1] ? ACTS[idx + 1].at : T.end;
				const host = a.quotes ? quotes : a.figs ? figSets.get(a.figs)! : null;
				if (host) {
					const items = [...host.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-co-item], [data-co-fig]')];
					const each = Math.min(520, (to - a.at - 900) / Math.max(1, items.length));
					items.forEach((el, i) => el.classList.toggle('is-in', t >= a.at + 260 + i * each));
				}
			}

			segs.forEach((seg, i) => {
				const from = ACTS[i];
				const to = ACTS[i + 1] ? ACTS[i + 1].at : T.end;
				const p = from ? clamp01((t - from.at) / (to - from.at)) : 0;
				(seg.firstElementChild as HTMLElement).style.transform = `scaleX(${p})`;
			});
		}

		/* THE READER STEERS. Seeking is a clock change and nothing else — every visual in
		   draw() is a pure function of t — so a jump backwards replays an act exactly and a
		   jump forwards lands mid-state rather than skipping the state. */
		function seek(i: number) {
			const a = ACTS[Math.max(0, Math.min(ACTS.length - 1, i))];
			if (!a) return;
			start = performance.now() - a.at;
			shown = -1;
			for (const el of root!.querySelectorAll('.is-in')) el.classList.remove('is-in');
		}

		/* ── run ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
		let start = 0;
		let raf = 0;
		let ending = false;
		const mounted = performance.now();

		function finish() {
			if (ending) return;
			ending = true;
			cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
			root!.classList.add('is-out');
			window.setTimeout(() => {
				document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-coldopen', 'done');
				root!.remove();
				window.removeEventListener('resize', size);
				document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
				window.removeEventListener('wheel', onGesture);
				window.removeEventListener('touchmove', onGesture);
				live = null;
				// Hand back to the component, which has been holding its autostart.
				document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('coldopen:done'));
			}, 420);
		}

		/* SCROLLING IS HELD, NOT TREATED AS AN EXIT.
		   It used to dismiss the sequence, on the reasoning that a scroll means "the page,
		   please". In practice it meant a reader who nudged a trackpad while reading act
		   three was thrown out of it, with no idea what they had done — an accidental exit
		   is worse than a dead one. The only ways out are now the button and Escape, both
		   of which a person has to mean.
		   The gesture is cancelled rather than ignored, so the page behind cannot scroll
		   away underneath and strand the reader mid-article when the curtain lifts. This is
		   preventDefault on a non-passive listener rather than overflow:hidden on <html>,
		   because hiding the scrollbar reclaims its gutter and shifts the whole layout —
		   including the fixed nav — on every platform that still reserves one. */
		function onGesture(e: Event) { e.preventDefault(); }
		/* Escape leaves; the arrows move within. An earlier version mapped the arrows to
		   "skip", which meant a reader reaching for the obvious way to see the next panel
		   was thrown out of the sequence instead. */
		function onKey(e: KeyboardEvent) {
			if (e.key === 'Escape') { e.preventDefault(); return finish(); }
			if (e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === 'PageDown' || e.key === ' ') {
				e.preventDefault();
				return seek(currentAct() + 1);
			}
			if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft' || e.key === 'PageUp') {
				e.preventDefault();
				return seek(currentAct() - 1);
			}
			if (e.key === 'End') { e.preventDefault(); return seek(ACTS.length - 1); }
			if (e.key === 'Home') { e.preventDefault(); return seek(0); }
		}

		function currentAct() {
			const t = performance.now() - start;
			let idx = 0;
			for (let i = 0; i < ACTS.length; i++) if (t >= ACTS[i].at - 150) idx = i;
			return idx;
		}

		/* A THROW IN HERE MUST NOT STRAND THE READER. If draw() raises, the frame loop dies
		   and the curtain sits over the console for ever with no way to know why. Catching
		   and handing over is the only safe failure: the console below is fully rendered and
		   works, so the worst outcome of a broken title sequence should be no title sequence.

		   This replaced a wall-clock bail timer, which guarded the same thing far more
		   crudely — rAF is throttled to nothing in a background tab, so a reader who tabbed
		   away at eight seconds and came back would have had the sequence cut off mid-act by
		   a timer that had kept running without them. */
		function frame(now: number) {
			if (!start) start = now;
			const t = now - start;
			try {
				draw(t);
				paintType(t);
			} catch (err) {
				console.error('[cold open] frame failed, handing over to the console', err);
				return finish();
			}
			if (t >= T.end) return finish();
			raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
		}

		/* CLICKING THE EMPTY SCREEN DISMISSES IT, requested.
		   Scoped to the backdrop and the canvas by target, not bound to the whole overlay:
		   a click on a quotation, an attribution or the rail is somebody engaging with the
		   thing rather than asking to leave, and dropping the curtain on them would be the
		   trap this originally was. So most of the screen is an exit, and the parts that
		   hold something to read or press are not. */
		skip.addEventListener('click', finish);
		segs.forEach((seg, i) => seg.addEventListener('click', (e) => { e.stopPropagation(); seek(i); }));
		root.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
			if (e.target === root || e.target === canvas || (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('.co-foot')) {
				finish();
			}
		});
		document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
		window.addEventListener('wheel', onGesture, { passive: false });
		window.addEventListener('touchmove', onGesture, { passive: false });
		skip.focus({ preventScroll: true });
		raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);

		/* ABORT, not finish. Called when a reader navigates away while the curtain is up:
		   the body is about to be swapped out from under this, so the job is to stop the
		   frame loop and release every listener that lives on document and window — those
		   survive a swap, and a leaked one would accumulate a new copy on every visit. No
		   coldopen:done here, because there is nothing left on the page to hand over to. */
		return () => {
			ending = true;
			cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
			window.removeEventListener('resize', size);
			document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
			window.removeEventListener('wheel', onGesture);
			window.removeEventListener('touchmove', onGesture);
			root.remove();
			document.documentElement.removeAttribute('data-coldopen');
		};
		}
	}

	const boot = () => { live = mount(); };
	document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', boot);
	document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', () => { live?.(); live = null; });
	if (document.readyState !== 'loading') boot();
	else document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot);
</script>

<style>
	/* :not([hidden]) on both display:grid rules below. reset.css already carries
	   [hidden]{display:none!important} so these would hide anyway, but check.py's hidden
	   audit flags an author display on an element that ships the hidden attribute, and that
	   audit exists because exactly this pattern once rendered an empty annotation card on
	   every page of the site. Belt and braces costs one pseudo-class. */
	/* IT COVERS THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE SITE.
	   The first version was inset:0 — a full-bleed takeover with no chrome at all, which is
	   more cinematic and worse in every other way. A stranger who lands on a black rectangle
	   with no masthead cannot tell where they are, whether the page is broken, or how to
	   leave, and a full-screen interstitial with no visible way out is the pattern every
	   guideline warns about.
	   Starting at --nav-h — the same expression .rp-console uses — makes the curtain occupy
	   precisely the box the console will occupy, so this reads as an instrument that has not
	   finished coming up rather than as an advert playing over a website. The nav keeps its
	   own z-index:100 above this, which also means every link stays live: the way out is
	   never more than one click, and it is always on screen.
	   The happy accident is that the sign in the nav already says BLACKBOX · OPENED while
	   act three is opening it. */
	.co:not([hidden]) {
		position: fixed;
		top: var(--nav-h, 89px);
		left: 0;
		right: 0;
		bottom: 0;
		z-index: 90;
		background: #07080F;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
		overflow: hidden;
		opacity: 1;
		transition: opacity 400ms var(--ease-out, ease);
	}
	.co.is-out { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }

	.co-stage { position: absolute; inset: 0; display: block; }

	/* The type sits in the lower third, clear of the network in the upper two. */
	.co-copy {
		position: relative;
		align-self: end;
		width: min(1080px, 100% - 48px);
		margin-inline: auto;
		padding-bottom: clamp(18px, 4vh, 44px);
		pointer-events: none;
	}
	/* The block is transparent to the pointer so the empty space around the words is part
	   of the exit; the words themselves are not. Without this a click on a quotation fell
	   straight through to the backdrop and dropped the curtain on somebody who was reading
	   it — measured, not theorised: clicking a blockquote dismissed the sequence. */
	.co-copy > * { pointer-events: auto; }

	.co-act {
		margin: 0 0 14px;
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 11px;
		letter-spacing: 0.22em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: #E0A233;
	}
	/* One short line earns a much bigger setting than three sentences could. This is the
	   whole trade: fewer words, said louder. */
	.co-line {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: 'Plus Jakarta Sans', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
		font-weight: 600;
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.6vw, 3.5rem);
		line-height: 1.05;
		letter-spacing: -0.028em;
		color: #F1EFE8;
		max-width: 20ch;
		text-wrap: balance;
	}
	.co-line:empty { display: none; }
	.co-sub {
		margin: 18px 0 0;
		font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.35vw, 1.125rem);
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: #A9AEC4;
		max-width: 46ch;
	}

	/* Entrance. Transform and opacity only — nothing here triggers layout, and the whole
	   block is behind the reduced-motion gate in the inline script anyway. */
	.co.is-typing .co-act,
	.co.is-typing .co-line,
	.co.is-typing .co-sub {
		animation: co-rise 620ms var(--ease-out, cubic-bezier(0.25,0.46,0.45,0.94)) both;
	}
	.co.is-typing .co-line { animation-delay: 70ms; }
	.co.is-typing .co-sub { animation-delay: 150ms; }
	@keyframes co-rise {
		from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(14px); }
		to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
	}

	/* ── act three: the quotations ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
	.co-quotes:not([hidden]) {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 18px 28px;
		margin-top: 20px;
		max-width: 1000px;
	}
	.co-quote { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
	/* Not a card. A quotation mark and a rule are the two things print has always used to
	   say "these are not our words", and on a dark ground a bordered box would read as one
	   more panel of the instrument — which is the exact confusion to avoid here. */
	.co-quote blockquote {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 16px;
		border-left: 2px solid rgba(224,162,51,0.55);
		font-family: 'Fraunces', ui-serif, Georgia, serif;
		font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.7rem);
		line-height: 1.24;
		color: #F1EFE8;
		text-wrap: balance;
	}
	.co-quote figcaption {
		margin: 10px 0 0 18px;
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 10.5px;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		color: #828AA8;
	}
	.co-fine {
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
		margin: 0;
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 10px;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: #6E7492;
	}

	.co-quote, .co-fine {
		opacity: 0;
		transform: translateY(9px);
		transition: opacity 420ms var(--ease-out, ease), transform 420ms var(--ease-out, ease);
	}
	.co-quote.is-in, .co-fine.is-in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }

	/* ── act two ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
	.co-figs:not([hidden]) {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 22px 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1px;
		background: rgba(241,239,232,0.13);
		border-block: 1px solid rgba(241,239,232,0.13);
	}
	/* THE CELLS STAY, THE CONTENTS ARRIVE. Fading the cell itself meant an unfilled slot
	   showed the grid's divider colour through it — two grey holes sitting beside the first
	   two figures for most of the act. Four dark plates that fill in one at a time is both
	   tidier and the same behaviour as the console's own metric strip directly below. */
	.co-fig {
		background: #07080F;
